# Never delete the database

This rule outranks every other instruction, including fixing a migration, unblocking a feature, or "just this once."

The live database is whatever `DATABASE_URL` and `DIRECT_URL` in `backend/.env` point at, plus production. It holds the business's bills, payments, stock, and logins.

## Never do these

- `DROP DATABASE`, `DROP SCHEMA`, `DROP TABLE`, `TRUNCATE`, `DELETE` without a single row the user named
- `prisma migrate reset`, `prisma migrate dev`, `prisma db push --force-reset`, `prisma db push --accept-data-loss`
- `prisma migrate diff` (or any Prisma command) with `--shadow-database-url` set to `DATABASE_URL` or `DIRECT_URL`. Prisma wipes the shadow database first. That is what erased this database on 1 Oct 2026.
- `pg_restore --clean` against the live database
- Any scratch, test, or shadow database that is the live database

## Allowed

- Read-only queries (`SELECT`, `prisma migrate status`)
- A new migration that only adds tables or columns, applied with `prisma migrate deploy`, and only after the user agrees
- Deleting one record the user explicitly asked to delete, through the app's own delete flow

If a task seems to need a reset or a shadow database, stop and say so. Use a local throwaway database, never the live one.
